<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Noun Phrases - Adjectives and modifiers</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>More than one adjective</h2>
    <p>A noun phrase can add different details: the <strong>tall, crooked</strong> tree; the <strong>muddy black</strong> dog.</p>
    <p>
      Adjectives may show size, colour, shape, age, feeling or another quality. “The <strong>small,
      fluffy</strong> puppy” adds size and texture to <em>puppy</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      More adjectives are not always better. “The big, large, enormous, huge dog” repeats the same idea.
      Choose details that help the reader identify or imagine the noun.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>A noun can modify another noun</h2>
    <p>Do not assume every word before a noun is an adjective. Ask: <strong>What is the main thing?</strong></p>
    <p>In “a <strong>chocolate</strong> cake,” <em>cake</em> is the head noun. <em>Chocolate</em> says what kind.</p>
    <p>In “the <strong>school</strong> bus,” <em>bus</em> is the head noun. <em>School</em> says what it is for.</p>
    <p>In <em>a garden wall</em>, the main thing is a wall. <em>Garden</em> says what sort.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Adjective or noun modifier?</h2>
      <p>
        a <strong>wooden</strong> box
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Adjective">✓</span>
        <em>Wooden</em> is an adjective.
      </p>
      <p>
        a <strong>wood</strong> box
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Noun modifier">✓</span>
        <em>Wood</em> is a noun naming the material. <em>Box</em> is still the head noun.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
